{title:Title}

{phang}
{bf:cegen} {hline 2} C-accelerated parallel extensions to generate


{marker syntax}{...}
{title:Syntax}

{p 8 17 2}
{cmd:cegen}
[{it:{help data types:type}}]
{newvar}
{cmd:=}
{it:fcn}{cmd:(}{it:arguments}{cmd:)}
{ifin}
[{cmd:,} {it:options}]

{p 8 17 2}
{cmd:by} {varlist}{cmd::}
{cmd:cegen} {it:...}

{synoptset 24 tabbed}{...}
{synopthdr}
{synoptline}
{syntab:By-group options}
{synopt:{opt by(varlist)}}compute the function within groups defined by {it:varlist}{p_end}

{syntab:Function options}
{synopt:{it:fcn_options}}options of the function; see {help cegen##functions:Functions}{p_end}

{syntab:Performance options}
{synopt:{opt thr:eads(#)}}maximum number of threads to use{p_end}
{synopt:{opt v:erbose}}display timing breakdown{p_end}
{synoptline}
{p 4 6 2}
{opt by(varlist)} and the {cmd:by} prefix are allowed with the functions marked
(by) below. {opt by()} needs no sort; the prefix needs the data sorted by
{it:varlist}, as every {cmd:by} prefix does.


{marker description}{...}
{title:Description}

{* ctools-credit:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{pstd}
{cmd:cegen} reimplements Stata's {cmd:egen} ({cmd:egen.ado} 3.4.2, April 13,
2020). See {help cegen##acknowledgments:Acknowledgments} for details.
{* ctools-credit:end}{...}

{pstd}
{cmd:cegen} creates {newvar} from a function of the data, with the syntax,
functions, options, storage types, labels, and messages of {help egen}. The
functions are computed by the ctools plugin: the sample is read, the function
evaluated, and the new variable written in parallel, and by-groups are found
without sorting the data in Stata. The order of the observations never
changes.

{pstd}
An {it:arguments} of {it:exp} is any Stata expression. A variable name is
read as it is; any other expression is first evaluated by {cmd:generate},
within by-groups when it refers to {cmd:_n}, {cmd:_N}, a subscript, or
{cmd:sum()}.


{marker functions}{...}
{title:Functions}

{dlgtab:Statistics of an expression (by)}

{pstd}
Each gives every observation of the sample the statistic of the nonmissing
values of {it:exp} in the sample, or in the observation's by-group.

{phang2}{opt count(exp)} number of nonmissing values; {it:exp} may be a string{p_end}
{phang2}{opt mean(exp)} mean{p_end}
{phang2}{opt total(exp)} [{cmd:,} {opt m:issing}] sum; 0 when no value is nonmissing, or
missing with {opt missing}{p_end}
{phang2}{opt sd(exp)} standard deviation{p_end}
{phang2}{opt min(exp)} [{cmd:,} {opt miss:ing}] and {opt max(exp)} [{cmd:,} {opt miss:ing}]
minimum and maximum; with {opt missing}, missing values are compared like
other values, above every number{p_end}
{phang2}{opt median(exp)} median{p_end}
{phang2}{opt pctile(exp)} [{cmd:,} {opt p(#)}] #th percentile, 0 < # < 100; the
default is {cmd:p(50)}{p_end}
{phang2}{opt iqr(exp)} interquartile range{p_end}
{phang2}{opt mad(exp)} median absolute deviation from the median{p_end}
{phang2}{opt mdev(exp)} mean absolute deviation from the mean{p_end}
{phang2}{opt skew(exp)} and {opt kurt(exp)} skewness and kurtosis{p_end}

{pstd}
These give each observation its own value:

{phang2}{opt std(exp)} [{cmd:,} {opt m:ean(#)} {opt s:d(#)}] {it:exp} standardized
to mean {cmd:mean()} (default 0) and standard deviation {cmd:sd()} (default 1){p_end}
{phang2}{opt pc(exp)} [{cmd:,} {opt prop}] {it:exp} as a percentage of its total,
or as a proportion with {opt prop}{p_end}
{phang2}{opt rank(exp)} [{cmd:,} {opt f:ield}|{opt t:rack}|{opt u:nique}] rank,
ties sharing their mean rank. {opt field} ranks the highest value 1 and gives
ties the lowest rank of the tie; {opt track} does the same from the lowest
value; {opt unique} gives ties distinct ranks, in observation order{p_end}
{phang2}{opt mode(varname)} [{cmd:,} {opt minmode}|{opt maxmode}|{opt n:ummode(#)} {opt miss:ing}]
most frequent value of a numeric or string variable, missing when several
values tie. {opt minmode} and {opt maxmode} pick the lowest and highest of
tied modes, {opt nummode(#)} the #th in ascending order; {opt missing} counts
missing values as a value{p_end}
{phang2}{opt seq()} [{cmd:,} {opt f:rom(#)} {opt t:o(#)} {opt b:lock(#)}] integers
from {cmd:from()} (default 1) to {cmd:to()} (default {cmd:_N}), each repeated
{cmd:block()} times, restarting in every by-group{p_end}

{dlgtab:Functions across variables}

{pstd}
Each is computed within an observation from the variables of {it:varlist}.

{phang2}{opt rowtotal(varlist)} [{cmd:,} {opt m:issing}] sum, missing values counting
as 0; with {opt missing}, missing when every variable is missing{p_end}
{phang2}{opt rowmean(varlist)} and {opt rowsd(varlist)} mean and standard deviation of
the nonmissing values{p_end}
{phang2}{opt rowmin(varlist)} and {opt rowmax(varlist)} minimum and maximum{p_end}
{phang2}{opt rowmedian(varlist)} and {opt rowpctile(varlist)} [{cmd:,} {opt p(#)}]
median and #th percentile{p_end}
{phang2}{opt rowfirst(varlist)} and {opt rowlast(varlist)} first and last nonmissing
value{p_end}
{phang2}{opt rowmiss(varlist)} and {opt rownonmiss(varlist)} [{cmd:,} {opt s:trok}]
number of missing and nonmissing values; string variables are allowed in
{cmd:rowmiss()}, and in {cmd:rownonmiss()} with {opt strok}{p_end}
{phang2}{opt anycount(varlist)}{cmd:,} {opt v:alues(integer numlist)} number of variables
equal to a listed value{p_end}
{phang2}{opt anymatch(varlist)}{cmd:,} {opt v:alues(integer numlist)} 1 if a variable
equals a listed value, else 0{p_end}
{phang2}{opt anyvalue(varname)}{cmd:,} {opt v:alues(integer numlist)} the variable
where it equals a listed value, else missing{p_end}
{phang2}{opt diff(varlist)} 1 if the variables are not all equal, else 0{p_end}

{dlgtab:Groups, bins, and sequences}

{phang2}{opt group(varlist)} [{cmd:,} {opt m:issing} {opt autotype} {opt l:abel}[{cmd:(}{it:lblname}[{cmd:,} {opt replace} {opt t:runcate(#)}]{cmd:)}]]
numbers the distinct values of {it:varlist} 1, 2, ... in sort order.
Observations with a missing value are left out unless {opt missing} is
given. {opt label} defines a value label holding each group's values;
{opt autotype} stores the result in the smallest integer type that holds it{p_end}
{phang2}{opt tag(varlist)} [{cmd:,} {opt m:issing}] 1 for the first observation of
each distinct value of {it:varlist}, else 0{p_end}
{phang2}{opt cut(varname)}{cmd:,} {c -(}{opt at(numlist)}|{opt g:roup(#)}{c )-} [{opt ic:odes} {opt l:abel}]
the left end of the {cmd:at()} interval holding {it:varname}, or with
{opt icodes} the interval's number from 0; {opt group(#)} cuts at the
quantiles that make # groups of about equal size. {opt label} labels the
codes{p_end}
{phang2}{opt fill(numlist)} continues the pattern of {it:numlist} down the
dataset; {cmd:if}, {cmd:in}, and {cmd:by} are not allowed{p_end}
{phang2}{opt ma(exp)} [{cmd:,} {opt t(#)} {opt nom:iss}] centered moving average of
{cmd:t()} observations (odd, default 3); {cmd:if} is not allowed{p_end}

{dlgtab:Strings}

{phang2}{opt concat(varlist)} [{cmd:,} {opt p:unct(str)} {opt d:ecode} {opt maxl:ength(#)} {opt f:ormat(%fmt)}]
joins the variables into one string, separated by {cmd:punct()}; numeric
variables are converted as {cmd:string()} converts them, in {cmd:format()}
when given, or to their value labels with {opt decode}{p_end}
{phang2}{opt ends(strvar)} [{cmd:,} {opt p:unct(str)} {opt tr:im} {opt h:ead}|{opt l:ast}|{opt t:ail}]
the part of {it:strvar} before the first {cmd:punct()} (default a blank),
after the first ({opt tail}), or after the last ({opt last}){p_end}

{dlgtab:Other functions}

{pstd}
The names {cmd:egen} kept from before Stata 9 are accepted: {cmd:sum()},
{cmd:any()}, {cmd:eqany()}, {cmd:neqany()}, {cmd:rfirst()}, {cmd:rlast()},
{cmd:rmax()}, {cmd:rmean()}, {cmd:rmin()}, {cmd:rmiss()}, {cmd:robs()},
{cmd:rsd()}, and {cmd:rsum()}.

{pstd}
{cmd:mtr()} and functions installed from other packages (for example
{cmd:egenmore}) are run by {cmd:egen} itself: {cmd:cegen} passes the command
on unchanged. See {help cegen##remarks:Remarks}.


{marker options}{...}
{title:Options}

{dlgtab:By-group options}

{phang}
{opt by(varlist)} computes the function separately within the groups that
{it:varlist} defines. Missing by-values form groups, each missing code its
own. Unlike the {cmd:by} prefix, {opt by()} does not need sorted data and
does not sort it.

{dlgtab:Performance options}

{phang}
{opt threads(#)} sets the maximum number of threads, from 1 to 256.
The default follows the {help ctools##threads:ctools thread setting}.
Results do not depend on the number of threads.

{phang}
{opt verbose} displays the sample size, the number of groups, the storage
type of the new variable, and elapsed times.


{marker remarks}{...}
{title:Remarks}

{pstd}
{bf:Results.} {cmd:cegen} follows the arithmetic of {cmd:egen} as well as its
definitions. With by-groups, {cmd:egen} adds up each group in the order of
the observations; {cmd:cegen} does the same, so on data sorted by the
by-variables the two give identical values. On unsorted data {cmd:egen}
sorts first, and Stata's sort orders ties at random, so sums there can differ
in the last digits from one run of {cmd:egen} to the next; {cmd:cegen} always
adds in observation order and gives the same value every time. Without
by-groups {cmd:egen} takes means and sums from {cmd:summarize}; {cmd:cegen}
uses compensated summation there, and the two agree to rounding error.
Percentiles follow {cmd:egen}'s two rules: {cmd:_pctile}'s without by-groups
and the formula of {cmd:egen}'s by-group code with them. The two rules differ
for a few combinations of group size and percentile (for example the 33.3rd
percentile of 3,000 values).

{pstd}
{bf:Storage types.} The new variable has the type {cmd:egen} gives it. A
{it:type} before {newvar} is used as given by most functions. Functions that
{cmd:egen} builds with {cmd:replace} ({cmd:rowtotal()}, {cmd:rowmax()},
{cmd:seq()}, {cmd:cut()}, {cmd:fill()}, and others) widen an integer type
that cannot hold a result, as {cmd:replace} does. {cmd:anycount()},
{cmd:anymatch()}, {cmd:tag()}, and {cmd:seq()} start from {cmd:byte};
{cmd:mode()} takes the type of its variable; {cmd:concat()} and {cmd:ends()}
create a string as wide as its longest value.

{pstd}
{bf:Ties in rank(), unique.} {cmd:egen} breaks ties in the order its sort
leaves them, which varies between runs. {cmd:cegen} breaks them in observation
order.

{pstd}
{bf:seq() with by and if.} {cmd:egen} numbers the observations of a by-group
in the order its sort leaves them when {cmd:if} is given. {cmd:cegen} numbers
them in observation order, which is what {cmd:egen} gives on data sorted by
the by-variables.

{pstd}
{bf:sd() of very large values by groups.} When the squares of the values
overflow (beyond about 1e154 in absolute value), {cmd:egen}'s running
{cmd:sum()} skips them as missing values and reports a wrong standard
deviation within by-groups (0 for values near 1e306). {cmd:cegen} gives
missing.

{pstd}
{bf:What egen still does.} {cmd:cegen} runs {cmd:egen} itself, with the same
arguments, in these cases: {cmd:mtr()} (its tax tables belong to Stata and
are not copied into ctools); functions that do not ship with Stata;
{cmd:rowfirst()} and {cmd:rowlast()} of string variables; a result or key
that needs a {cmd:strL} longer than 2,045 bytes; a word before {newvar} that
is not a storage type; a function given no arguments (other than
{cmd:seq()} and {cmd:group()}). In {cmd:concat()}, numbers
other than integers of up to seven digits, and any number with {cmd:format()}
or {opt decode}, are converted to text by Stata's {cmd:string()} and
{cmd:decode} before the plugin joins them, so the text is always Stata's.
The undocumented {cmd:suffix()} option of {cmd:rank()} is not supported.

{pstd}
{bf:Expressions.} An expression that is not a variable name costs one
{cmd:generate} over the data before the plugin runs. Pass a variable where
you can.


{marker examples}{...}
{title:Examples}

{pstd}Group means without sorting:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. sysuse auto, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen mean_price = mean(price), by(foreign)}{p_end}

{pstd}Several by-variables, an expression, and a subsample:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen double share = pc(price * 1.1) if mpg > 15, by(foreign rep78)}{p_end}

{pstd}Medians and percentiles within groups:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen med = median(price), by(rep78)}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen p90 = pctile(price), p(90) by(rep78)}{p_end}

{pstd}Ranks within groups, highest first:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen r = rank(mpg), field by(foreign)}{p_end}

{pstd}Group identifiers with a value label, and one flag per group:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen long id = group(foreign rep78), label}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen first = tag(foreign rep78)}{p_end}

{pstd}Row statistics:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen total = rowtotal(price weight length)}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen nmiss = rowmiss(rep78 mpg)}{p_end}

{pstd}Quintile groups and intervals:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen q = cut(price), group(5)}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen band = cut(mpg), at(10 20 30 50) label}{p_end}

{pstd}Strings:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen brand = ends(make), head}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen key = concat(foreign rep78), punct("-")}{p_end}

{pstd}With the {cmd:by} prefix and an expression that depends on the group:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. bysort foreign (price): cegen run = total(price[_n-1])}{p_end}

{pstd}Timing breakdown on four threads:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cegen sd_price = sd(price), by(foreign) threads(4) verbose}{p_end}


